    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d398">

                <pb facs="0309" n="305" sameAs="frus:frus1938v04/pg305"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.73/133a: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the First Secretary of Embassy in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Salisbury</hi>), <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >at Peiping</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-04-11T15:00:00-05:00">April 11, 1938—3 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>103. Reference Tsingtao’s monthly political report for January, 1938,<anchor
                        n="93" xml:id="d398-f1"/> page 15, which states that “telegrams despatched
                    from Tsingtao must be in the Japanese language”.</p>
                <p>If this regulation is still in effect, it is suggested that you instruct the
                    Consul at Tsingtao to consult with interested colleagues on the advisability of
                    making informal representations to the appropriate authorities to the end that
                    the telegraph administration accept for transmission telegrams in the English
                    language.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
